window.TOPICS=window.TOPICS||{};window.TOPICS['gmm']={"id": "gmm", "title": "GMM and EM, plus regression and GD", "tagline": "Mixture likelihood, E-step responsibilities, M-step updates, EM versus Lloyd, and linear regression with gradient descent. 18 PYQs (58.5 marks) solved step by step.", "concepts": {"jensen": {"name": "Jensen inequality", "marks": 4, "ease": 3, "repair": "<p>Draw a bowl-shaped curve and pick two points on it. The straight chord between them lies above the curve: the average of the heights is at least the height at the average. That is Jensen. The weights must be a proper average: non-negative and adding to $1$.</p>"}, "gmm": {"name": "GMM likelihood and E-step", "marks": 42.5, "ease": 2, "repair": "<p>A responsibility is Bayes rule in one line: weight times density for this component, divided by the same thing summed over all components. Pick the density table rows carefully, multiply by $\\pi$, then normalise so the numbers add to $1$.</p>"}, "em": {"name": "EM and K-means", "marks": 6, "ease": 2, "repair": "<p>E-step: freeze the parameters and work out soft memberships $\\gamma$. M-step: freeze the memberships and recompute each component as a weighted proportion, weighted mean and weighted variance. Repeat. The likelihood never drops, but the end point depends on the start.</p>"}, "gd": {"name": "Linear regression and gradient descent", "marks": 6, "ease": 3, "repair": "<p>For linear regression the squared error is a bowl with one bottom, so gradient descent with a small enough step reaches the global minimum. Each step moves against the gradient: $w\\leftarrow w-\\eta\\nabla L$. Too large a step oscillates or diverges.</p>"}}, "sections": [{"id": "orient", "title": "Orientation: mixtures, EM and the Jensen link", "html": "<p>A Gaussian mixture says: each point came from one of $K$ Gaussian groups, but we do not know which. EM alternates between guessing the groups (softly) and refitting the Gaussians.</p><div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"bt\">Where this sits</div><p>K-means gave hard groups. GMM gives soft ones: each point has a probability of belonging to every group. EM is the general tool for models with hidden variables.</p></div><h3>Prerequisite check</h3><details class=\"rc\"><summary>Bayes rule</summary><p>$P(A\\mid B)=P(B\\mid A)P(A)/P(B)$.</p></details><details class=\"rc\"><summary>Gaussian density</summary><p>$N(x;\\mu,\\sigma^2)=\\frac1{\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-(x-\\mu)^2/2\\sigma^2}$.</p></details><h3>PYQ sources</h3><p>Converted from the Quiz 1 notes. Teaching, widgets and worked examples stay on the linked Quiz 1 pages; every PYQ below is solved here in the Quiz 2 format with each option explained. [FLAG] and [?] mark gaps or mismatches in the source.</p><ul><li><a href=\"#q1-b25\">6.0 to 6.2 Jensen, mixture likelihood, E-step</a></li><li><a href=\"#q1-b26\">6.3 to 6.4 M-step, EM and gradient descent</a></li></ul>"}, {"id": "jen", "title": "6.0 Jensen inequality and EM versus K-means", "html": "<div class=\"box formula\"><div class=\"bt\">Jensen</div><p>For a convex $f$ and weights $\\lambda_i\\ge0$ with $\\sum\\lambda_i=1$: $f(\\sum\\lambda_ia_i)\\le\\sum\\lambda_if(a_i)$. Both conditions are needed. (For a concave function such as $\\ln$ the inequality reverses; this is how EM shows the likelihood does not decrease.)</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 6</div><p>$(0.5,0.25,0.25)$ and $(\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13)$ are valid. $(1,-1,1)$ has a negative weight; $(10,-6,-4)$ does not sum to $1$.</p></div><div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"bt\">EM versus Lloyd</div><p>With $\\sigma^2\\to0$ and equal weights, EM's soft assignments become hard and the M-step for $\\mu_k$ is Lloyd's mean update. Both monotonically improve their objective, reach only a local optimum, and depend on initialisation.</p></div>", "quiz": [{"id": "gq5", "concept": "jensen", "level": "easy", "type": "mcq", "stem": "$f(x)=x^2$, weights $(0.5,0.5)$, points $0$ and $2$. Compare $f(\\text{average})$ with the average of $f$.", "options": [{"t": "$f(1)=1\\le2=$ average of $f$", "ok": true, "why": "Correct: Jensen for convex $f$."}, {"t": "$f(1)=1\\ge2$", "ok": false, "why": "Reversed."}, {"t": "They are equal", "ok": false, "why": "$1\\ne2$."}, {"t": "Cannot tell", "ok": false, "why": "It can be computed."}]}, {"id": "gq6", "concept": "jensen", "level": "medium", "type": "mcq", "stem": "For the concave $\\ln$, Jensen says", "options": [{"t": "$\\ln(\\sum\\lambda_ia_i)\\ge\\sum\\lambda_i\\ln a_i$", "ok": true, "why": "Correct: the inequality reverses for concave functions; EM uses this bound."}, {"t": "$\\ln(\\sum\\lambda_ia_i)\\le\\sum\\lambda_i\\ln a_i$", "ok": false, "why": "That is the convex direction."}, {"t": "equality always", "ok": false, "why": "Only for equal $a_i$."}, {"t": "nothing", "ok": false, "why": "It applies."}]}]}, {"id": "lik", "title": "6.1 Mixture density and likelihood", "html": "<div class=\"box formula\"><div class=\"bt\">Mixture density</div>$$p(x)=\\sum_{k=1}^K\\pi_k\\,N(x;\\mu_k,\\sigma_k^2),\\qquad \\pi_k\\ge0,\\ \\sum_k\\pi_k=1$$<p>$\\pi_k$ is the prior probability of group $k$ (mixing weight). The hidden label $z$ is summed out. For iid data, $L=\\prod_i\\sum_k\\pi_kN(x_i;\\mu_k,\\sigma_k^2)$ and $\\ell=\\sum_i\\ln\\sum_k\\pi_kN(x_i;\\cdot)$. The sum stays inside the log, so there is no closed-form maximiser: this is why EM is needed.</p></div><div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"bt\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Writing $\\sum_i\\sum_k$ for the likelihood.</li><li>Forgetting the weights $\\pi_k$.</li><li>Splitting $\\ln(a+b)$ into $\\ln a+\\ln b$.</li></ul></div>", "quiz": [{"id": "gq1", "concept": "gmm", "level": "easy", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Which is the density of a two-component Gaussian mixture?", "options": [{"t": "$\\pi_1N(x;\\mu_1,\\sigma_1^2)+\\pi_2N(x;\\mu_2,\\sigma_2^2)$", "ok": true, "why": "Correct."}, {"t": "$N(x;\\mu_1,\\sigma_1^2)N(x;\\mu_2,\\sigma_2^2)$", "ok": false, "why": "A product is not a mixture."}, {"t": "$N(x;\\pi_1\\mu_1+\\pi_2\\mu_2,1)$", "ok": false, "why": "That is one Gaussian."}, {"t": "$\\max_kN(x;\\mu_k,\\sigma_k^2)$", "ok": false, "why": "Not a density."}]}]}, {"id": "estep", "title": "6.2 E-step: responsibilities", "html": "<div class=\"box formula\"><div class=\"bt\">Responsibility</div>$$\\gamma_{ik}=P(z_i=k\\mid x_i)=\\frac{\\pi_kf_k(x_i)}{\\sum_j\\pi_jf_j(x_i)},\\qquad \\sum_k\\gamma_{ik}=1$$<p>It is Bayes rule: prior $\\pi_k$ times how well component $k$ explains $x_i$, divided by the total. For one point the responsibilities of all components add to $1$.</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 1 (simple)</div><p>$\\pi=(0.6,0.4)$, $f_1(x)=0.2$, $f_2(x)=0.5$: $0.12$ and $0.20$, total $0.32$; $\\gamma=(0.375,0.625)$.</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 2 (exam level): reading a table</div><p>$\\pi=(0.3,0.7)$, component 1 is $(\\mu,\\sigma^2)=(0,1)$, component 2 is $(1,2)$, point $x=1.2$ with table values $0.194$ and $0.279$. $\\gamma_1=\\dfrac{0.3\\cdot0.194}{0.3\\cdot0.194+0.7\\cdot0.279}=\\dfrac{0.0582}{0.2535}=0.230$. Match each component to its own row of the table.</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 3 (tricky): equal weights and variances</div><p>$\\pi=(0.5,0.5)$, $\\mu=(1,3)$, $\\sigma^2=1$, $x=1$: $\\gamma_1=\\dfrac1{1+e^{-2}}=0.881$ (a sigmoid of the squared-distance difference over $2\\sigma^2$).</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 4: posterior odds</div><p>Posterior odds $=$ likelihood ratio $\\times$ prior odds. If $f_1=3f_2$ and $\\gamma_1=0.6$ (odds $1.5$), prior odds are $0.5$, so $\\pi_1=1/3$. If you are given $\\pi_1=0.2$ and $\\gamma_1=0.8$ you can find the ratio $f_1/p$ ($=4$) and the odds, but not $p(x)$ itself.</p></div><div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"bt\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Dropping $\\pi$ (using only the densities).</li><li>Mixing up rows of the density table.</li><li>Confusing prior $\\pi_k$ (about the whole dataset) with posterior $\\gamma_{ik}$ (about one point).</li></ul></div><div class=\"explore\"><div class=\"bt\">Interactive explorer</div><p>Move the probe $x$ and the parameters: the curve shows $\\gamma_1(x)$, the responsibility of component 1.</p><div class=\"q1 q1w\"><div data-widget=\"gmmresp\" data-pi=\"0.3\" data-m1=\"0\" data-m2=\"1\" data-v1=\"1\" data-v2=\"2\" data-x=\"1.2\"></div></div></div>", "quiz": [{"id": "gq2", "concept": "gmm", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "$\\pi=(0.5,0.5)$, $f_1(x)=0.3$, $f_2(x)=0.1$. $\\gamma_1$?", "num": {"ans": 0.75, "tol": 0.001, "show": "0.75"}, "explain": "$0.15/(0.15+0.05)$.", "hint": ""}]}, {"id": "mstep", "title": "6.3 M-step and the EM loop", "html": "<div class=\"box formula\"><div class=\"bt\">M-step (with responsibilities fixed)</div><p>$N_k=\\sum_i\\gamma_{ik}$ (soft count). $$\\pi_k=\\frac{N_k}n,\\quad\\mu_k=\\frac{\\sum_i\\gamma_{ik}x_i}{N_k},\\quad\\sigma_k^2=\\frac{\\sum_i\\gamma_{ik}(x_i-\\mu_k)^2}{N_k}.$$ These are the weighted versions of the usual proportion, mean and variance.</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 5</div><p>$x=(0,2,4)$, $\\gamma_k=(0.5,0.5,1.0)$. $N_k=2$, $\\pi_k=2/3$, $\\mu_k=(0+1+4)/2=2.5$, $\\sigma_k^2=(0.5\\cdot6.25+0.5\\cdot0.25+1\\cdot2.25)/2=2.75$.</p></div><p><b>EM loop:</b> initialise $\\theta$; E-step computes $\\gamma$ with $\\theta$ fixed; M-step updates $\\theta$ with $\\gamma$ fixed; repeat. The likelihood never decreases, but EM can stop at a local maximum, so the start matters.</p><div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"bt\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Dividing by $n$ instead of $N_k$ in $\\mu_k$ and $\\sigma_k^2$.</li><li>Swapping E and M.</li><li>Believing EM finds the global maximum.</li></ul></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 6 (exam level): one full EM iteration</div><p>Data $0,1,4,5$; $K=2$; start $\\pi=(0.5,0.5)$, $\\mu=(0,5)$, $\\sigma^2=1$ for both. <b>E-step</b> (equal weights and variances, so $\\gamma_1=1/(1+e^{-((x-5)^2-x^2)/2})$): $\\gamma_1=(1.0000, 0.9994, 0.0006, 0.0000)$. <b>M-step:</b> $N_1=2.0000$, so $\\pi_1=0.5000$; $\\mu_1=0.5008$ and $\\mu_2=4.4992$. The means move from $0,5$ to about $0.5$ and $4.5$, the centres of the two natural groups.</p></div><div class=\"explore\"><div class=\"bt\">Interactive explorer</div><p>Change the responsibilities and watch the weighted mean, variance and mixing weight update. Set every $\\gamma$ to $0$ or $1$ to get the K-means update.</p><div class=\"q1 q1w\"><div data-widget=\"mstep\" data-x=\"1,2,6\" data-g=\"0.9,0.8,0.1\"></div></div></div>", "quiz": [{"id": "gq3", "concept": "em", "level": "medium", "type": "num", "stem": "$x=(2,4,6)$, $\\gamma_k=(1,0.5,0)$. New $\\mu_k$? (3 decimals)", "num": {"ans": 2.667, "tol": 0.002, "show": "2.667"}, "explain": "$N_k=1.5$; $(2+2+0)/1.5=2.667$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "gq4", "concept": "em", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "Same responsibilities $(1,0.5,0)$, $n=3$. New $\\pi_k$?", "num": {"ans": 0.5, "tol": 0.001, "show": "0.5"}, "explain": "$1.5/3$.", "hint": ""}]}, {"id": "gdsec", "title": "6.5 Final: linear regression and gradient descent", "html": "<p>The last Quiz 1 week links back to regression. These PYQs are listed under this sub-heading in the PYQ section below; the full treatment is in the <a href=\"#t-linear-regression\">Linear regression and gradient descent</a> topic.</p><div class=\"box formula\"><div class=\"bt\">Least squares and gradient descent</div><p>Model $y=w^Tx$. Loss $L(w)=\\sum_i(w^Tx_i-y_i)^2$. Gradient $\\nabla L=2\\sum_i(w^Tx_i-y_i)x_i$. Step: $w\\leftarrow w-\\eta\\nabla L$. $L$ is convex, so any local minimum is global; if some $w$ makes every residual zero, it is optimal.</p></div><div class=\"box worked\"><div class=\"bt\">Worked example 7</div><p>$f(w)=w^2$, $w_0=1$. Gradient $2w$. $\\eta=0.25$: $w_1=1-0.25\\cdot2=0.5$, then $0.25$, halving each step. $\\eta=0.5$: $w_1=0$ in one step. $\\eta=1$: $w$ flips between $1$ and $-1$. $\\eta>1$: it diverges.</p></div><div class=\"explore\"><div class=\"bt\">Interactive explorer</div><p>Change the step size and watch gradient descent converge, zigzag, oscillate or diverge.</p><div class=\"q1 q1w\"><div data-widget=\"gd1d\" data-L=\"4\" data-w0=\"1\" data-eta=\"0.25\"></div></div></div>", "quiz": [{"id": "gq7", "concept": "gd", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "$f(w)=w^2$, $w_0=1$, $\\eta=0.25$. $w_1$?", "num": {"ans": 0.5, "tol": 0.001, "show": "0.5"}, "explain": "$1-0.25\\cdot2$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "gq8", "concept": "gd", "level": "medium", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Why does gradient descent on least squares reach the global minimum (small enough step)?", "options": [{"t": "The loss is convex", "ok": true, "why": "Correct."}, {"t": "The data is centred", "ok": false, "why": "Not needed."}, {"t": "The gradient is constant", "ok": false, "why": "It is not."}, {"t": "It uses random samples", "ok": false, "why": "That is SGD."}]}, {"id": "gq9", "concept": "gd", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "Model $y=w^Tx$ with $w=(1,-2)$. Prediction for $x=(3,1)$?", "num": {"ans": 1, "tol": 0.001, "show": "1"}, "explain": "$3-2$.", "hint": ""}]}, {"id": "pyqs", "title": "All PYQs of this topic, solved", "kind": "pyq", "html": ""}, {"id": "rev", "title": "Revision page", "html": "<h3>Formulas</h3><table><tr><th>Item</th><th>Formula</th></tr><tr><td>Responsibility</td><td>$\\pi_kf_k/\\sum_j\\pi_jf_j$</td></tr><tr><td>$\\pi_k$</td><td>$\\tfrac1n\\sum_i\\gamma_{ik}$</td></tr><tr><td>$\\mu_k$</td><td>$\\sum\\gamma x/\\sum\\gamma$</td></tr><tr><td>Log-likelihood</td><td>$\\sum_i\\ln\\sum_k\\pi_kf_k(x_i)$</td></tr></table><h3>Exam decision flow</h3><ol><li>E-step: Bayes rule per point.</li><li>M-step: weighted proportion, mean, variance.</li><li>MAP assignment: pick the larger $\\gamma$ (threshold $0.5$ for two components).</li></ol>"}, {"id": "test", "title": "Topic test: mixed questions", "kind": "test", "html": ""}, {"id": "checklist", "title": "Checklist: skills you should be able to do", "kind": "checklist", "html": ""}], "pyqs": [{"id": "gm01", "exam": "Jul 2026 (16 Jul)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q7", "marks": 4, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "jensen", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "6.0 Jensen inequality", "wording": "Let $f:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ be a convex function. Which choices of $(\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2,\\lambda_3)$ guarantee that $f(\\lambda_1a_1+\\lambda_2a_2+\\lambda_3a_3)\\le\\lambda_1f(a_1)+\\lambda_2f(a_2)+\\lambda_3f(a_3)$ holds for every convex function $f$ and every $a_1,a_2,a_3\\in\\mathbb R$?", "options": ["$(-1,1,1)$", "$(0.5,0.25,0.25)$", "$(1,-1,1)$", "$(10,-6,-4)$", "$\\left(\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13\\right)$"], "key": {"opts": [1, 4]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> which weight triples make Jensen hold for every convex $f$.</p>", "idea": "<p>Jensen needs the weights to form a probability vector: every $\\lambda_i\\ge0$ and $\\sum\\lambda_i=1$. A negative weight or a wrong sum breaks the inequality for some convex $f$.</p>", "method": ["Option 1 $(-1,1,1)$: the sum is $1$ but one weight is negative. Take $f(x)=x^2$ and $a=(1,0,0)$. The weighted point is $-1$, so the LHS is $f(-1)=1$. The RHS is $-1\\cdot f(1)+1\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(0)=-1$. Since $1>-1$, Jensen fails.", "Option 2 $(0.5,0.25,0.25)$: all weights $\\ge0$ and they sum to $1$. Valid.", "Option 3 $(1,-1,1)$: sum $1$, one negative weight. Take $f(x)=x^2$ and $a=(0,1,0)$. The weighted point is $-1$, so the LHS is $1$; the RHS is $1\\cdot0-1\\cdot1+1\\cdot0=-1$. Fails.", "Option 4 $(10,-6,-4)$: the weights sum to $0$, not $1$. With the constant convex function $f\\equiv1$ the LHS is $1$ and the RHS is $10-6-4=0$. Fails.", "Option 5 $(\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13)$: valid."], "others": "<p>Both conditions are needed: sum $=1$ alone (options 1, 3) is not enough, and equal weights are not required (option 2).</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: 20000 random $a\\in[-5,5]^3$ and $f\\in\\{x^2,1,-1,|x|,e^x\\}$: options 1, 3, 4 fail, options 2 and 5 never fail.</p>", "trap": "Checking only \"sum $=1$\" and picking options 1 and 3, or accepting only equal weights.", "rule": "<p>Jensen holds iff $\\lambda_i\\ge0$ and $\\sum\\lambda_i=1$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Which of (a) $(0.1,0.2,0.7)$ (b) $(0.6,0.6,-0.2)$ (c) $(0.4,0.4,0.4)$ (d) $(1,0,0)$ guarantee Jensen?", "a": "(a) and (d). (b) has a negative weight ($f=x^2$, $a=(0,0,1)$: LHS $0.04$, RHS $-0.2$). (c) sums to $1.2$ ($f\\equiv-1$: LHS $-1$, RHS $-1.2$)."}, "optWhy": ["Negative weight: fails.", "<b>Valid.</b>", "Negative weight: fails.", "Sum is $0\\ne1$ and has negative weights: fails.", "<b>Valid.</b>"]}}, {"id": "gm02", "exam": "Oct 2025 (26 Oct)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q15", "marks": 3, "type": "SA", "concept": "gmm", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "6.2 E-step responsibilities", "tagNote": "The data-point index in \"$\\lambda_1^?$\" is missing; the key matches $x=1$ [?].", "context": "Cluster 1-D data $x_1=1,\\ x_2=10,\\ x_3=20$ with a mixture of 2 Gaussians using EM (the source text gives $\\sigma=20$ and omits $x_3$ in the context line; $x_3=20$ is read from the density table).", "wording": "Suppose at the $t$-th iteration of the EM algorithm, the parameters are $\\pi_1=0.3,\\ \\pi_2=0.7$, $\\mu_1=2,\\ \\mu_2=3$, $\\sigma_1^2=1,\\ \\sigma_2^2=1$. The densities of the data points given each mixture component: $x=1$: $f(x\\mid z=1)=0.242$, $f(x\\mid z=2)=0.054$; $x=10$: $0.399$, $0.242$; $x=20$: $0.242$, $0.399$. What is the value of $\\lambda_1^?$ ? Enter the answer correct to two decimal places.", "key": {"lo": 0.64, "hi": 0.68, "show": "0.64 to 0.68"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the responsibility $\\lambda_1$ (the superscript lost its index in the source).</p>", "idea": "<p>E-step: $\\gamma_1=\\dfrac{\\pi_1f_1}{\\pi_1f_1+\\pi_2f_2}$ (Bayes rule). The key range matches the point $x=1$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$\\pi_1f_1=0.3\\times0.242=0.0726$.</li><li>$\\pi_2f_2=0.7\\times0.054=0.0378$.</li><li>$\\gamma_1=\\dfrac{0.0726}{0.0726+0.0378}=\\dfrac{0.0726}{0.1104}=0.6576\\approx0.66$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Other rows give $\\gamma_1(10)=0.414$ and $\\gamma_1(20)=0.206$, outside the range.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $(0.6576,0.4140,0.2063)$ for $x=(1,10,20)$.</p>", "trap": "Dropping $\\pi$: $0.242/(0.242+0.054)=0.818$, wrong. Using the wrong row.", "rule": "<p>$\\gamma_k=\\pi_kf_k/\\sum_j\\pi_jf_j$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$\\pi=(0.4,0.6)$, one point with $f_1=0.5$, $f_2=0.2$. $\\gamma_1,\\gamma_2$?", "a": "$0.20$ and $0.12$; $\\gamma_1=0.625$, $\\gamma_2=0.375$."}}}, {"id": "gm03", "exam": "Oct 2025 (26 Oct)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q14", "marks": 3, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "gmm", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "6.1 Mixture likelihood", "tagNote": "Only the correct option is preserved in the source; the other three options are reconstructed typical wrong forms [?].", "context": "Cluster 1-D data $x_1=1,\\ x_2=10,\\ x_3=20$ with a mixture of 2 Gaussians using EM (the source text gives $\\sigma=20$ and omits $x_3$ in the context line; $x_3=20$ is read from the density table).", "wording": "Which among the following represents the likelihood function $L(\\theta)$ for GMM? Here $\\theta$ is the set of parameters $\\mu_1,\\mu_2,\\sigma_1,\\sigma_2,\\pi_1,\\pi_2$.", "options": ["$L(\\theta;x_1,x_2,x_3)=\\prod_{i=1}^{3}\\Big[\\sum_{k=1}^{2}\\pi_k\\frac1{\\sigma_k\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-\\frac12\\left(\\frac{x_i-\\mu_k}{\\sigma_k}\\right)^2}\\Big]$", "$L(\\theta;x_1,x_2,x_3)=\\sum_{i=1}^{3}\\Big[\\sum_{k=1}^{2}\\pi_k\\frac1{\\sigma_k\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-\\frac12\\left(\\frac{x_i-\\mu_k}{\\sigma_k}\\right)^2}\\Big]$", "$L(\\theta;x_1,x_2,x_3)=\\prod_{i=1}^{3}\\Big[\\prod_{k=1}^{2}\\pi_k\\frac1{\\sigma_k\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-\\frac12\\left(\\frac{x_i-\\mu_k}{\\sigma_k}\\right)^2}\\Big]$", "$L(\\theta;x_1,x_2,x_3)=\\prod_{i=1}^{3}\\Big[\\sum_{k=1}^{2}\\frac1{\\sigma_k\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-\\frac12\\left(\\frac{x_i-\\mu_k}{\\sigma_k}\\right)^2}\\Big]$"], "key": {"opts": [0]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the GMM likelihood for three points.</p>", "idea": "<p>Each point has density $p(x_i)=\\sum_k\\pi_kN(x_i;\\mu_k,\\sigma_k^2)$ (the hidden label is summed out), and independent points multiply.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$L(\\theta;x_1,x_2,x_3)=\\prod_{i=1}^{3}\\Big[\\sum_{k=1}^{2}\\pi_k\\dfrac1{\\sigma_k\\sqrt{2\\pi}}\\exp\\!\\Big(-\\dfrac12\\Big(\\dfrac{x_i-\\mu_k}{\\sigma_k}\\Big)^2\\Big)\\Big]$ (option 1).</li></ol>", "others": "<p>The log of this likelihood keeps a sum inside the log, which is why EM is needed.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: table values $p=(0.1104,0.2891,0.3519)$, $L=0.011231$, $\\ln L=-4.489$.</p>", "trap": "Using $\\sum_i\\sum_k$, or dropping $\\pi_k$.", "rule": "<p>Mixture likelihood $=\\prod_i\\sum_k\\pi_kf_k(x_i)$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Write the log-likelihood of iid data from $\\pi\\,\\text{Pois}(\\lambda_1)+(1-\\pi)\\text{Pois}(\\lambda_2)$.", "a": "$\\ell=\\sum_i\\ln\\!\\big[\\pi\\tfrac{e^{-\\lambda_1}\\lambda_1^{x_i}}{x_i!}+(1-\\pi)\\tfrac{e^{-\\lambda_2}\\lambda_2^{x_i}}{x_i!}\\big]$; the sum stays inside the log."}, "optWhy": ["<b>Correct</b> (the only option shown in the source).", "Reconstructed distractor: sum over $i$ of sums, treating the likelihood as a sum of densities. Wrong.", "Reconstructed distractor: product over points of a product over components, which multiplies the two Gaussians together instead of mixing them. Wrong.", "Reconstructed distractor: omits the weights $\\pi_k$, so the mixture is not a density. Wrong."]}}, {"id": "gm04", "exam": "Mar 2026 (15 Mar)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q20", "marks": 2, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "gmm", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "6.1 Mixture likelihood", "tagNote": "Only the correct option is preserved in the source; the others are reconstructed typical wrong forms [?].", "context": "$X_1,\\dots,X_n$ are iid from the mixture $f(x\\mid\\theta)=\\theta f_1(x)+(1-\\theta)f_2(x)$, $0<\\theta<1$, with $f_1(x)=e^{-x}$, $f_2(x)=3e^{-3x}$ for $x\\ge0$. Observed data $x=\\{0.2,0.5,1.1,1.8\\}$; initial estimate $\\theta^{(0)}=0.6$.", "wording": "Which expression correctly represents the log-likelihood function $\\ell(\\theta)$ for the given data?", "options": ["$\\ell(\\theta)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\log\\big[\\theta e^{-x_i}+(1-\\theta)\\,3e^{-3x_i}\\big]$", "$\\ell(\\theta)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\big[\\log(\\theta e^{-x_i})+\\log((1-\\theta)\\,3e^{-3x_i})\\big]$", "$\\ell(\\theta)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\log\\big[\\theta e^{-x_i}+(1-\\theta)e^{-3x_i}\\big]$", "$\\ell(\\theta)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\log\\big[\\theta e^{-x_i}+\\theta\\,3e^{-3x_i}\\big]$"], "key": {"opts": [0]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the mixture log-likelihood.</p>", "idea": "<p>Log of the product of mixture densities, with the sum kept inside each log.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$\\ell(\\theta)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\log\\big[\\theta f_1(x_i)+(1-\\theta)f_2(x_i)\\big]$.</li><li>Substitute: $\\ell(\\theta)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\log\\big[\\theta e^{-x_i}+(1-\\theta)\\,3e^{-3x_i}\\big]$ (option 1).</li></ol>", "others": "<p>The factor $3$ in $f_2=3e^{-3x}$ matters: without it $f_2$ is not a density.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $\\ell(0.6)=-3.9881$; the incorrect splitting gives $-15.714$.</p>", "trap": "Forgetting the $3$, or breaking $\\log(a+b)$ into $\\log a+\\log b$.", "rule": "<p>$\\ell=\\sum_i\\log\\sum_k\\pi_kf_k(x_i)$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$f=\\theta N(x;0,1)+(1-\\theta)N(x;3,1)$, data $\\{0,2\\}$, $\\theta=0.5$. Value of $\\ell$?", "a": "$x=0$: $0.2017$; $x=2$: $0.1480$; $\\ell=-1.601-1.911=-3.512$."}, "optWhy": ["<b>Correct</b> (the only option kept in the source).", "Reconstructed distractor: splits the log of the sum into a sum of logs, which is not valid.", "Reconstructed distractor: drops the factor $3$ in $f_2$.", "Reconstructed distractor: puts $\\theta$ on both components."]}}, {"id": "gm05", "exam": "Jul 2023", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q12", "marks": 4.5, "type": "SA", "concept": "gmm", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "6.2 E-step responsibilities", "wording": "Consider a GMM with two components: $\\pi_1=0.3,\\ \\pi_2=0.7$; $\\mu_1=0,\\ \\sigma_1^2=1$; $\\mu_2=1,\\ \\sigma_2^2=2$. What is the probability that the point $x=1.2$ comes from the first component? Use the table: $N(1.2;\\mu{=}0,\\sigma^2{=}1)=0.194$, $N(1.2;0,2)=0.197$, $N(1.2;1,1)=0.391$, $N(1.2;1,2)=0.279$. Enter your answer correct to three decimal places.", "key": {"lo": 0.15, "hi": 0.35, "show": "0.15 to 0.35"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the posterior probability that $x=1.2$ came from component 1.</p>", "idea": "<p>Pick the right two table entries: component 1 is $(\\mu,\\sigma^2)=(0,1)$, component 2 is $(1,2)$. Then Bayes rule.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$N_1=0.194$ (from $\\mu=0,\\sigma^2=1$); $N_2=0.279$ (from $\\mu=1,\\sigma^2=2$).</li><li>$\\pi_1N_1=0.3\\times0.194=0.0582$; $\\pi_2N_2=0.7\\times0.279=0.1953$.</li><li>$\\gamma_1=\\dfrac{0.0582}{0.0582+0.1953}=\\dfrac{0.0582}{0.2535}=0.2296\\approx0.230$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>$\\gamma_1<\\pi_1$ because the data favours component 2 ($0.194<0.279$).</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $0.22959$; $\\gamma_2=0.7704$.</p>", "trap": "Using rows $(0,2)$ and $(1,1)$ gives $0.178$; dropping the weights gives $0.410$.", "rule": "<p>Match each component with its own $(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ row, then weight by $\\pi$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$\\pi=(0.6,0.4)$, $N_1=0.2$, $N_2=0.5$. $\\gamma_1$?", "a": "$0.12/(0.12+0.20)=0.375$."}}}, {"id": "gm06", "exam": "Feb 2025 (23 Feb)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q11", "marks": 4, "type": "SA", "concept": "gmm", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "6.2 E-step responsibilities", "wording": "Suppose we fit a Gaussian Mixture Model with $K=2$ for $D=\\{x_1=1,x_2=3,x_3=2,x_4=5\\}$. At the beginning of the $t$-th E-step: $\\pi_1=0.5,\\pi_2=0.5$; $\\mu_1=1,\\mu_2=3$; $\\sigma_1^2=1,\\sigma_2^2=1$. What is the probability that $x_1$ belongs to the first component? Enter the answer correct to two decimal places.", "key": {"lo": 0.86, "hi": 0.9, "show": "0.86 to 0.90"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> $\\gamma_1$ for $x_1=1$.</p>", "idea": "<p>Equal weights and equal variances make the common factors cancel, leaving a sigmoid of the squared-distance difference.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$f_1\\propto e^{-(1-1)^2/2}=1$; $f_2\\propto e^{-(1-3)^2/2}=e^{-2}=0.1353$.</li><li>$\\gamma_1=\\dfrac{1}{1+e^{-2}}=\\dfrac1{1.1353}=0.8808\\approx0.88$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Only $x_1$ is asked; the other points are not used.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $0.88080$, $\\gamma_2=0.1192$.</p>", "trap": "Forgetting the $\\tfrac12$ gives $1/(1+e^{-4})=0.982$.", "rule": "<p>Equal $\\pi,\\sigma^2$: $\\gamma_1=\\dfrac{1}{1+\\exp\\!\\big(-\\tfrac{(x-\\mu_2)^2-(x-\\mu_1)^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\big)}$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$\\pi=(0.5,0.5)$, $\\mu=(0,2)$, $\\sigma^2=1$, $x=0.5$. $\\gamma_1$?", "a": "Log odds $=(2.25-0.25)/2=1$, $\\gamma_1=1/(1+e^{-1})=0.731$."}}}, {"id": "gm07", "exam": "Mar 2026 (15 Mar)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q21", "marks": 3, "type": "SA", "concept": "gmm", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "6.2 E-step responsibilities", "context": "$X_1,\\dots,X_n$ are iid from the mixture $f(x\\mid\\theta)=\\theta f_1(x)+(1-\\theta)f_2(x)$, $0<\\theta<1$, with $f_1(x)=e^{-x}$, $f_2(x)=3e^{-3x}$ for $x\\ge0$. Observed data $x=\\{0.2,0.5,1.1,1.8\\}$; initial estimate $\\theta^{(0)}=0.6$.", "wording": "Introduce latent variables $Z_i\\in\\{0,1\\}$; if $Z_i=1$, $X_i$ is generated from $f_1$. Compute the posterior responsibility $\\gamma_i^{(0)}=P(Z_i=1\\mid X_i=x_i,\\theta^{(0)})$. Find the aggregate $\\sum_i\\gamma_i^{(0)}$ (answer to two decimal places).", "key": {"lo": 2.74, "hi": 2.8, "show": "2.74 to 2.80"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> $\\sum_{i=1}^{4}\\gamma_i^{(0)}$.</p>", "idea": "<p>Here the mixing weights are $\\theta=0.6$ and $1-\\theta=0.4$. $\\gamma_i=\\dfrac{\\theta f_1(x_i)}{\\theta f_1(x_i)+(1-\\theta)f_2(x_i)}$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$x=0.2$: $0.6e^{-0.2}=0.4912$; $0.4\\cdot3e^{-0.6}=0.6586$; $\\gamma=0.4272$.</li><li>$x=0.5$: $0.6e^{-0.5}=0.3639$; $1.2e^{-1.5}=0.2678$; $\\gamma=0.5761$.</li><li>$x=1.1$: $0.6e^{-1.1}=0.1997$; $1.2e^{-3.3}=0.0443$; $\\gamma=0.8186$.</li><li>$x=1.8$: $0.6e^{-1.8}=0.0992$; $1.2e^{-5.4}=0.0054$; $\\gamma=0.9482$.</li><li>Sum $=0.4272+0.5761+0.8186+0.9482=2.7701\\approx2.77$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Each $\\gamma\\in(0,1)$, so the sum is in $(0,4)$; $2.77/4=0.69$ exceeds the prior $0.6$ because the data lean to component 1.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $2.77013$.</p>", "trap": "Dropping the $3$ in $f_2$, or reporting the mean $0.6925$ instead of the sum.", "rule": "<p>E-step: Bayes rule per point; the sum of $\\gamma_i$ is the expected count in component 1.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Same mixture, $\\theta=0.5$, $x=0.4$. $\\gamma$?", "a": "Odds $=e^{2x}/3=e^{0.8}/3=0.7418$, $\\gamma=0.426$."}}}, {"id": "gm08", "exam": "Mar 2026 (15 Mar)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q22", "marks": 3, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "gmm", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "6.2 E-step responsibilities", "tagNote": "Options 3 and 4 are identical in the source copy; the real option 4 is unrecoverable [?].", "context": "$X_1,\\dots,X_n$ are iid from the mixture $f(x\\mid\\theta)=\\theta f_1(x)+(1-\\theta)f_2(x)$, $0<\\theta<1$, with $f_1(x)=e^{-x}$, $f_2(x)=3e^{-3x}$ for $x\\ge0$. Observed data $x=\\{0.2,0.5,1.1,1.8\\}$; initial estimate $\\theta^{(0)}=0.6$.", "wording": "Using a MAP decision rule, a data point is assigned to mixture 2 if $\\gamma_i^{(0)}\\le0.5$. Based on the E-step values, which data point(s) are classified as belonging to Component 2?", "options": ["$\\{0.2\\}$ only", "$\\{0.2,0.5\\}$ only", "$\\{1.1,1.8\\}$ only", "$\\{1.1,1.8\\}$ only"], "key": {"opts": [0]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the points with $\\gamma\\le0.5$.</p>", "idea": "<p>Use the responsibilities from the previous question: $\\gamma=(0.4272,\\ 0.5761,\\ 0.8186,\\ 0.9482)$ for $x=(0.2,0.5,1.1,1.8)$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$\\gamma\\le0.5$ only for $x=0.2$ ($0.4272$). The others exceed $0.5$ and go to component 1.</li><li>Answer: $\\{0.2\\}$ only (option 1).</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Switch point: $\\gamma=0.5\\iff e^{-x}\\cdot0.6=0.4\\cdot3e^{-3x}$, i.e. $x^*=\\tfrac12\\ln2=0.3466$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $\\gamma=[0.4272,0.5761,0.8186,0.9482]$; $x^*=0.3466$.</p>", "trap": "Reading the rule backwards, or comparing $x$ (not $\\gamma$) to $0.5$.", "rule": "<p>MAP: pick the component with the larger posterior.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$\\theta=0.5$, data $\\{0.3,0.6,1.0\\}$. Which are in component 2?", "a": "Odds $=e^{2x}/3\\le1\\iff x\\le0.5493$. $\\gamma(0.3)=0.378$, $\\gamma(0.6)=0.525$, $\\gamma(1.0)=0.711$; only $0.3$."}, "optWhy": ["<b>Correct.</b>", "$x=0.5$ has $\\gamma=0.576>0.5$, so it belongs to component 1.", "These two are the clearly component-1 points.", "Identical to option 3 in the source copy (the real option 4 was lost)."]}}, {"id": "gm09", "exam": "Jul 2026 (16 Jul)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q9", "marks": 4, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "gmm", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "6.2 E-step responsibilities", "wording": "Consider a GMM with $K$ components. For a single data point $x$, for component 1 you are given $\\pi_1=0.2$ (prior mixing weight) and $\\gamma_1=0.8$ (posterior responsibility). Which quantities can be uniquely determined?", "options": ["The marginal probability density $p(x)$.", "The ratio $\\dfrac{f(x\\mid z=1)}{p(x)}$", "The ratio $\\dfrac{\\pi_1f(x\\mid z=1)}{\\sum_{k=2}^{K}\\pi_kf(x\\mid z=k)}$", "$\\sum_{k=2}^{K}\\gamma_k$"], "key": {"opts": [1, 2, 3]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> which quantities follow from $\\pi_1$ and $\\gamma_1$ alone.</p>", "idea": "<p>$\\gamma_1=\\dfrac{\\pi_1f_1}{p(x)}$ and $\\sum_k\\gamma_k=1$. The unknown $p(x)$ is free, so only ratios in which it cancels are fixed.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Option 1 ($p(x)$): $\\gamma_1=\\pi_1f_1/p$ has three unknowns ($f_1$, $p$ and the rest); scaling all densities by any constant keeps $\\gamma_1=0.8$ but changes $p$. Not determined.</li><li>Option 2 ($f_1/p$): $\\gamma_1/\\pi_1=0.8/0.2=4$. Determined.</li><li>Option 3 ($\\pi_1f_1/\\sum_{k\\ge2}\\pi_kf_k$): $\\gamma_1/(1-\\gamma_1)=0.8/0.2=4$. Determined (the common $p$ cancels).</li><li>Option 4 ($\\sum_{k\\ge2}\\gamma_k$): $=1-\\gamma_1=0.2$. Determined.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Scenario A ($p=0.5$) and scenario B ($p=1.0$) both have $\\gamma_1=0.8$ and $\\pi_1=0.2$, so $p$ is not fixed; the ratios $4,4,0.2$ are the same in both.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code reproduces the two scenarios.</p>", "trap": "Rejecting option 3 because $p$ is unknown (it cancels), or accepting option 1.", "rule": "<p>$\\gamma_1/\\pi_1=f_1/p$, odds $=\\pi_1f_1/\\sum_{k\\ge2}\\pi_kf_k$, $\\sum_k\\gamma_k=1$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$\\pi_1=0.25$, $\\gamma_1=0.5$. Which are determined: (a) $f_1/p$ (b) odds $\\gamma_1/(1-\\gamma_1)$ (c) $p(x)$ (d) $\\sum_{k\\ge2}\\gamma_k$?", "a": "(a) $2$, (b) $1$, (d) $0.5$ determined; (c) not."}, "optWhy": ["Not determined: scale invariance.", "<b>Determined</b> ($=4$).", "<b>Determined</b> ($=4$).", "<b>Determined</b> ($=0.2$)."]}}, {"id": "gm10", "exam": "Jul 2026 (16 Jul)", "year": "2026", "qno": "Q10", "marks": 4, "type": "SA", "concept": "gmm", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "6.2 E-step responsibilities", "wording": "Consider a two-component GMM with mixing weights $\\pi_1,\\pi_2$. For a particular observation $x_i$, the component conditional densities satisfy $f(x_i\\mid z_i=1)=3f(x_i\\mid z_i=2)$. During the E-step, the posterior responsibility of component 1 is $\\gamma_1=0.60$. Determine $\\pi_1$, correct to two decimal places.", "key": {"lo": 0.3, "hi": 0.36, "show": "0.30 to 0.36"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> $\\pi_1$.</p>", "idea": "<p>Write $\\gamma_1$ with $f_1=3f_2$ and solve for $\\pi_1$; $\\pi_2=1-\\pi_1$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$0.60=\\dfrac{3\\pi_1}{3\\pi_1+(1-\\pi_1)}=\\dfrac{3\\pi_1}{1+2\\pi_1}$.</li><li>$0.6(1+2\\pi_1)=3\\pi_1\\Rightarrow0.6+1.2\\pi_1=3\\pi_1\\Rightarrow\\pi_1=\\dfrac{0.6}{1.8}=\\dfrac13\\approx0.33$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Odds view: posterior odds $1.5=$ likelihood ratio $3\\times$ prior odds, so prior odds $=0.5$ and $\\pi_1=0.5/1.5=1/3$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $\\pi_1=0.3333$ reproduces $\\gamma_1=0.6$.</p>", "trap": "Solving with $\\pi_1=0.6$ or ignoring that $\\pi_2=1-\\pi_1$.", "rule": "<p>Posterior odds $=$ likelihood ratio $\\times$ prior odds.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$f_1=2f_2$, $\\gamma_1=0.5$. $\\pi_1$?", "a": "Prior odds $=1/2$, so $\\pi_1=1/3$."}}}, {"id": "gm11", "exam": "Oct 2023", "year": "2023", "qno": "Q8", "marks": 4, "type": "SA", "concept": "gmm", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "6.3 M-step and EM", "wording": "A GMM is fit for a dataset with 5 points. At some time-step in the EM algorithm, the values of $\\lambda_k^i$ for all points in the dataset for the $k^{th}$ mixture are: $\\lambda_k^1=0.3,\\ \\lambda_k^2=0.1,\\ \\lambda_k^3=0.5,\\ \\lambda_k^4=0.5,\\ \\lambda_k^5=0.3$. What is the estimate of $\\pi_k$ after the M-step? Enter the answer to two decimal places.", "key": {"lo": 0.3, "hi": 0.4, "show": "0.3 to 0.4"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the new mixing weight $\\pi_k$.</p>", "idea": "<p>M-step: $\\pi_k=\\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n\\lambda_k^i$, the average responsibility of component $k$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$\\sum_i\\lambda_k^i=0.3+0.1+0.5+0.5+0.3=1.7$.</li><li>$\\pi_k=1.7/5=0.34$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>$1.7$ is the expected number of points in component $k$; dividing by $n=5$ turns it into a fraction.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $0.34$; it lies between $\\min\\lambda=0.1$ and $\\max\\lambda=0.5$.</p>", "trap": "Reporting $1.7$ (a count), or dividing by $1.7$ or by $K$.", "rule": "<p>$\\pi_k^{new}=\\tfrac1n\\sum_i\\gamma_{ik}$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$n=8$, $\\lambda_k=0.9,0.7,0.2,0.1,0,0.4,0.6,0.1$. $\\pi_k^{new}$?", "a": "Sum $3.0$; $\\pi_k=3.0/8=0.375$."}}}, {"id": "gm12", "exam": "Jul 2024 (07 Jul)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q6", "marks": 3, "type": "SA", "concept": "gmm", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "6.3 M-step and EM", "wording": "The EM algorithm is run on a dataset of 100 points modeled using a GMM with three components. Let $\\lambda_k^i$ be the value obtained in the E-step for data point $i$ and component $k$. The estimated mixture probabilities are $\\pi_1=0.3,\\ \\pi_2=0.5,\\ \\pi_3=0.2$. For some data point $x_p$, $\\lambda_1^p+\\lambda_3^p=0.2$. What is the probability that the second component generated $x_p$, given that we have observed $x_p$?", "key": {"lo": 0.8, "hi": 0.8, "show": "0.8"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> $\\lambda_2^p=P(z_p=2\\mid x_p)$.</p>", "idea": "<p>For a single point the responsibilities of all components sum to $1$.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>$\\lambda_1^p+\\lambda_2^p+\\lambda_3^p=1$.</li><li>$\\lambda_2^p=1-0.2=0.8$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>$\\pi_2=0.5$ is the average of $\\lambda_2^i$ over all $100$ points; a single point can have $\\lambda_2^p=0.8\\ne0.5$ (posterior differs from prior).</p>", "verify": "<p>Check: $0.2+0.8=1$.</p>", "trap": "Answering $\\pi_2=0.5$ (prior instead of posterior), or multiplying things like $0.5\\times0.8$.", "rule": "<p>$\\sum_k\\gamma_{ik}=1$ for every point.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$K=4$; for a point $\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2=0.35$ and $\\lambda_4=0.15$. $\\lambda_3$?", "a": "$1-0.35-0.15=0.50$."}}}, {"id": "gm13", "exam": "Oct 2024 (27 Oct)", "year": "2024", "qno": "Q9", "marks": 4, "type": "SA", "concept": "gmm", "tag": "FLAG", "sub": "6.3 M-step and EM", "tagNote": "[FLAG] One weight is $2.5>1$, which a real E-step cannot give. Solved as printed; the key range holds.", "wording": "Consider a GMM for 5 points: $x_1=1,x_2=1.2,x_3=2,x_4=1.5,x_5=0.5$. At some timestep in EM, the values of $\\lambda_k^i$ for the $k$-th mixture after the E-step are: $\\lambda_k^1=0.3,\\lambda_k^2=0.1,\\lambda_k^3=2.5,\\lambda_k^4=0.6,\\lambda_k^5=0.8$. Find the estimate of $\\mu_k$ after the M-step.", "key": {"lo": 1.5, "hi": 1.6, "show": "1.50 to 1.60"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the new mean $\\mu_k$.</p>", "idea": "<p>M-step: $\\mu_k=\\dfrac{\\sum_i\\lambda_k^ix_i}{\\sum_i\\lambda_k^i}$, a weighted mean.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Numerator: $0.3(1)+0.1(1.2)+2.5(2)+0.6(1.5)+0.8(0.5)=0.3+0.12+5+0.9+0.4=6.72$.</li><li>Denominator: $0.3+0.1+2.5+0.6+0.8=4.3$.</li><li>$\\mu_k=6.72/4.3=1.5628\\approx1.56$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>[FLAG] $\\lambda_k^3=2.5>1$ is not a valid probability; the question is answered exactly as stated. The weight $2.5$ pulls $\\mu$ toward $x_3=2$ (plain mean $1.24$).</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $1.56279$.</p>", "trap": "Dividing by $n=5$ ($6.72/5=1.344$) or taking the plain mean $1.24$.", "rule": "<p>$\\mu_k=\\sum\\lambda x/\\sum\\lambda$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$x=(0,2,4)$, $\\lambda_k=(0.5,0.5,1.0)$. New $\\mu_k$ and $\\sigma_k^2$?", "a": "$N=2$; $\\mu=5/2=2.5$; $\\sigma^2=(0.5\\cdot6.25+0.5\\cdot0.25+1\\cdot2.25)/2=2.75$."}}}, {"id": "gm14", "exam": "Jul 2025 (13 Jul)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q6", "marks": 3, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "em", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "6.3 M-step and EM", "wording": "You are presented with a dataset that has latent variables influencing your data. You are asked to use the Expectation-Maximization (EM) algorithm to best capture the data. How would you define the steps in the EM algorithm?", "options": ["E-step: Estimate the latent variables in the dataset. M-step: Maximize the likelihood over the model parameters.", "E-step: Estimate the number of latent variables in the dataset. M-step: Maximize the likelihood over the model parameters.", "E-step: Estimate the likelihood over the model parameters. M-step: Maximize the number of latent variables in the dataset.", "E-step: Estimate the likelihood over the model parameters. M-step: Maximize the number of parameters in the model."], "key": {"opts": [0]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> what E and M steps do.</p>", "idea": "<p>E-step: with $\\theta$ fixed, compute the posterior $P(z\\mid x,\\theta)$ of the hidden variables. M-step: with those posteriors fixed, change $\\theta$ to maximise the (expected) likelihood.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Option 1 states exactly this.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>\"Estimate the latent variables\" means soft posteriors $\\gamma$, not hard guesses.</p>", "verify": "<p>Source key option 1.</p>", "trap": "Swapping E and M, or choosing an option that talks about \"the number of\".", "rule": "<p>E: $\\theta$ fixed, compute $\\gamma$. M: $\\gamma$ fixed, update $\\theta$.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "In iteration $t$, which quantity is fixed and which varies in the M-step? In the E-step?", "a": "M-step: $\\gamma$ fixed, $\\theta$ varies. E-step: $\\theta^{(t)}$ fixed, $\\gamma$ computed."}, "optWhy": ["<b>Correct.</b>", "The number of latent variables is never estimated by EM.", "Swaps the roles of the steps and \"maximises the number of latent variables\", which is meaningless.", "The number of parameters is fixed by the model, not maximised."]}}, {"id": "gm15", "exam": "Jul 2025 (13 Jul)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q7", "marks": 3, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "em", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "6.4 EM, K-means and gradient descent", "tagNote": "The source marks no correct option; key derived (options 2, 4, 5) [?].", "wording": "Which of the following statements is/are true?", "options": ["EM algorithm converges to a global optimum.", "Lloyd's algorithm converges to a local optimum.", "Poor initialization does not affect the clusters produced by EM algorithm.", "Poor initialization leads to a slower convergence of Lloyd's algorithm.", "Lloyd's algorithm is sensitive to the number of clusters $k$."], "key": {"opts": [1, 3, 4]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> which statements about EM and Lloyd\\'s algorithm hold.</p>", "idea": "<p>Both EM and Lloyd (K-means) improve their objective every step but only reach a local optimum; the result depends on the initialisation and on the chosen number of clusters.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Option 1 (EM converges to a global optimum): false. The likelihood never decreases, but it can stop at a local maximum.</li><li>Option 2 (Lloyd converges to a local optimum): true.</li><li>Option 3 (poor initialisation does not affect EM): false; different starts give different solutions.</li><li>Option 4 (poor initialisation slows Lloyd): true under the standard reading (bad starts need more iterations).</li><li>Option 5 (Lloyd is sensitive to $k$): true; the result and the objective change with $k$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Statements 1 to 3 are certain; 4 and 5 follow the standard course reading.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: EM from 5 starts reaches different likelihoods; Lloyd for $k=2,3,4$ gives different objectives; a poor start needs more iterations than a K-means++ start.</p>", "trap": "Jumping from \"EM increases the likelihood\" to \"EM finds the global maximum\".", "rule": "<p>EM and Lloyd: monotone, local optimum, depend on init.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "True or false: (a) EM never decreases $\\ell$. (b) Lloyd never increases $F$. (c) Two inits always give the same answer. (d) With $\\sigma^2\\to0$ and equal $\\pi$, EM behaves like Lloyd.", "a": "(a) True. (b) True. (c) False. (d) True (hard assignments)."}, "optWhy": ["False.", "<b>True.</b>", "False.", "<b>True</b> (standard reading).", "<b>True.</b>"]}}, {"id": "gm16", "exam": "Jul 2025 (13 Jul)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q17", "marks": 3, "type": "MCQ", "concept": "gd", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "6.5 Final: linear regression and gradient descent", "tagNote": "Notation \"$y=wx$\" read as $y=w^Tx$ [?]; this does not change the key.", "context": "Dataset with feature vector $x$ and label $y$: $[4\\ 1]^T\\to2$; $[2\\ 8]^T\\to-14$; $[1\\ 4]^T\\to-7$; $[3\\ 2]^T\\to-1$.", "wording": "Fit the linear regression model $y=wx$, $w=[w_1\\ w_2]^T$ using the squared error. Which model results?", "options": ["$y=x_1+2x_2$", "$y=x_1-2x_2$", "$y=x_1-x_2$", "$y=x_1+x_2$"], "key": {"opts": [1]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the least-squares $w$.</p>", "idea": "<p>If some $w$ fits all four points exactly, the squared error is $0$ (the minimum), so it is the best fit. Read $y=wx$ as $y=w^Tx$ (no bias).</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Test option 2, $w=(1,-2)$: point 1: $4-2=2$ ✓. Point 2: $2-16=-14$ ✓. Point 3: $1-8=-7$ ✓. Point 4: $3-4=-1$ ✓.</li><li>Zero error, so this is the global minimum: $y=x_1-2x_2$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>Normal equations $XX^Tw=Xy$ (with columns as points) give the same $w^*=(1,-2)$.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: $w^*=(1,-2)$, residuals all $0$; the other options have MSE $340,21.25,191.25$.</p>", "trap": "Checking only one point: an option can fit one point by luck, so test every point (here only option 2 fits all four). Also, using $X^TX$ ($4\\times4$) instead of $XX^T$ ($2\\times2$) in the normal equations.", "rule": "<p>Exact fit means zero loss, hence optimal.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$x_1=(1,2)\\to5$, $x_2=(2,1)\\to4$, $y=w^Tx$. Best $w$ and MSE?", "a": "$w_1+2w_2=5$, $2w_1+w_2=4$: $w=(1,2)$, MSE $0$."}, "optWhy": ["$(1,2)$: predictions $6,18,9,7$, MSE $340$.", "<b>Correct</b>, MSE $0$.", "$(1,-1)$: MSE $21.25$.", "$(1,1)$: MSE $191.25$."]}}, {"id": "gm17", "exam": "Jul 2025 (13 Jul)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q18", "marks": 1, "type": "SA", "concept": "gmm", "tag": "SRC", "sub": "6.5 Final: linear regression and gradient descent", "context": "Dataset with feature vector $x$ and label $y$: $[4\\ 1]^T\\to2$; $[2\\ 8]^T\\to-14$; $[1\\ 4]^T\\to-7$; $[3\\ 2]^T\\to-1$.", "wording": "Find the mean squared error for the training dataset.", "key": {"lo": 0, "hi": 0, "show": "0"}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> the training MSE of the fitted model.</p>", "idea": "<p>The fitted model $y=x_1-2x_2$ reproduces every label.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>All four residuals are $0$.</li><li>MSE $=0$.</li></ol>", "others": "<p>MSE $0$ means a perfect fit on the training data, not a guaranteed good model on new data.</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: MSE $=0.0$.</p>", "trap": "Computing the MSE for a wrong $w$ (for example $(1,-1)$ gives $21.25$).", "rule": "<p>Exact fit gives zero loss.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "Add $x_3=(1,1)\\to9$ to $x_1=(1,2)\\to5$, $x_2=(2,1)\\to4$. Is the best MSE still $0$?", "a": "No: $w^*=(17/11,28/11)$, $L=324/11$, MSE $=108/11=9.82$."}}}, {"id": "gm18", "exam": "Jul 2025 (13 Jul)", "year": "2025", "qno": "Q8", "marks": 3, "type": "MSQ", "concept": "gd", "tag": "DERIVED", "sub": "6.5 Final: linear regression and gradient descent", "tagNote": "The source marks no correct option; key derived (options 1 and 4) [?].", "wording": "For a linear regression problem, which among the following are true?", "options": ["Gradient descent will converge to a global minimum.", "Gradient descent will converge to a local minimum (which is not a global minimum).", "Stochastic gradient descent is guaranteed to converge to a global minimum.", "Gradient descent will take lesser number of iterations to converge to a local minimum than stochastic gradient descent."], "key": {"opts": [0, 3]}, "sol": {"asked": "<p><b>Asked:</b> which gradient-descent statements are true for linear regression.</p>", "idea": "<p>The squared-error loss is a convex quadratic, so any local minimum is the global minimum. Plain GD with a small enough step converges; SGD with a constant step keeps bouncing around.</p>", "method": "<ol><li>Option 1 (GD converges to a global minimum): true.</li><li>Option 2 (GD converges to a local minimum that is not global): false, convexity.</li><li>Option 3 (SGD guaranteed to converge to a global minimum): false; with a constant step it only hovers around the minimum (guarantee needs a shrinking step, which the question does not state).</li><li>Option 4 (GD takes fewer iterations than SGD): true under the usual reading (GD uses the exact gradient).</li></ol>", "others": "<p>GD must use a suitable $\\eta$: for $f(w)=w^2$ ($L=2$) with $w_0=1$, $\\eta=1$ oscillates ($-1,1,\\dots$), $\\eta=0.5$ lands on $0$, $\\eta=1.1$ diverges ($|w_1|=1.2$).</p>", "verify": "<p>Code: GD ($\\eta=0.05$) settles at $2.2143$; SGD tail standard deviations $0.102$ and $0.056$ remain non-zero.</p>", "trap": "Assuming GD converges for any $\\eta$, or taking the shrinking-step SGD theorem as given.", "rule": "<p>Convex loss: local $=$ global; constant-step SGD does not settle.</p>", "transfer": {"q": "$f(w)=w^2$, $w_0=1$: GD with $\\eta=1$, $0.5$, $1.1$. What are $w_1$?", "a": "$-1$ (oscillates), $0$, $-1.2$ (diverges)."}, "optWhy": ["<b>True.</b>", "False: no non-global local minima.", "False as stated.", "<b>True</b> (standard reading)."]}}], "test": [{"id": "t1", "concept": "gmm", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "$\\pi=(0.4,0.6)$, one point with $f_1=0.5$, $f_2=0.2$. $\\gamma_1$?", "num": {"ans": 0.625, "tol": 0.002, "show": "0.625"}, "explain": "$0.20/(0.20+0.12)=0.625$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "t2", "concept": "gmm", "level": "easy", "type": "num", "stem": "For one point, $\\gamma_1=0.35$, $\\gamma_4=0.15$, $K=4$, $\\gamma_2=0.2$. $\\gamma_3$?", "num": {"ans": 0.3, "tol": 0.001, "show": "0.3"}, "explain": "$1-0.35-0.2-0.15=0.3$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "t3", "concept": "em", "level": "medium", "type": "num", "stem": "Responsibilities of component $k$ for 5 points: $0.3,0.1,0.5,0.5,0.3$. New $\\pi_k$?", "num": {"ans": 0.34, "tol": 0.001, "show": "0.34"}, "explain": "$1.7/5$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "t4", "concept": "em", "level": "medium", "type": "num", "stem": "$x=(1,3)$, $\\gamma_k=(0.25,0.75)$. New $\\mu_k$?", "num": {"ans": 2.5, "tol": 0.001, "show": "2.5"}, "explain": "$(0.25+2.25)/1=2.5$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "t5", "concept": "em", "level": "medium", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Which is true about EM?", "options": [{"t": "It always reaches the global maximum of the likelihood", "ok": false, "why": "Local maxima are possible."}, {"t": "The likelihood never decreases between iterations", "ok": true, "why": "Correct."}, {"t": "The E-step updates $\\mu_k$", "ok": false, "why": "The M-step updates parameters."}, {"t": "Initialisation does not matter", "ok": false, "why": "It matters."}]}, {"id": "t6", "concept": "jensen", "level": "medium", "type": "mcq", "stem": "Jensen holds for every convex $f$ with weights", "options": [{"t": "$(2,-1,0)$", "ok": false, "why": "Negative weight."}, {"t": "$(0.2,0.3,0.5)$", "ok": true, "why": "Correct: non-negative and sum $1$."}, {"t": "$(0.5,0.5,0.5)$", "ok": false, "why": "Sum is $1.5$."}, {"t": "$(-0.5,0.75,0.75)$", "ok": false, "why": "Negative weight."}]}, {"id": "t7", "concept": "gmm", "level": "hard", "type": "num", "stem": "$f_1=3f_2$, $\\gamma_1=0.75$. $\\pi_1$?", "num": {"ans": 0.5, "tol": 0.001, "show": "0.5"}, "explain": "Odds $3=3\\times\\frac{\\pi_1}{1-\\pi_1}$, so prior odds $1$, $\\pi_1=0.5$.", "hint": ""}, {"id": "t8", "concept": "gmm", "level": "hard", "type": "mcq", "stem": "The GMM log-likelihood has no closed-form maximiser because", "options": [{"t": "the sum over components sits inside the log", "ok": true, "why": "Correct."}, {"t": "the Gaussians are not differentiable", "ok": false, "why": "They are smooth."}, {"t": "$\\pi_k$ must be positive", "ok": false, "why": "A constraint, not the obstacle."}, {"t": "$K$ is unknown", "ok": false, "why": "Even with known $K$ it is hard."}]}], "checklist": [{"id": "c1", "t": "I can compute a responsibility from priors and component densities."}, {"id": "c2", "t": "I know the M-step updates for pi, mu and sigma squared."}, {"id": "c3", "t": "I can say why EM and Lloyd reach only local optima."}], "formulas": [{"name": "Responsibility", "tex": "\\gamma_{ik}=\\frac{\\pi_kf_k(x_i)}{\\sum_j\\pi_jf_j(x_i)}", "note": ""}, {"name": "M-step", "tex": "\\pi_k=\\tfrac1n\\sum_i\\gamma_{ik},\\ \\mu_k=\\frac{\\sum\\gamma_{ik}x_i}{\\sum\\gamma_{ik}}", "note": ""}]};